{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01005 - MONDO_16249",
  "verbalization": "Hydroxyurea is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome through its association with ovarian tumors that are sensitive to cisplatin. The evidence suggests that Hydroxyurea may act as a potential therapeutic agent for cancers such as Maligant Granulosa Cell Tumor Of Ovary, Ovarian Endometrioid Adenocarcinoma, and Malignant Sex Cord Stromal Tumor Of Ovary, all of which are linked to cisplatin. These cancers are classified under the broader category of ovarian tumors that are responsive to cisplatin, which in turn is associated with Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome. The indirect pathway through cisplatin implies a possible mechanism where Hydroxyurea may influence tumor biology in a way that enhances cisplatin's efficacy, potentially through metabolic or cellular pathways that are shared among these tumor types. The cumulative evidence suggests a possible role for Hydroxyurea in the treatment of ovarian cancers that are associated with this syndrome, though further research is needed to elucidate the precise mechanism of action.",
